's invalid because after Action, we have Thought instead of Observation. So violation at line 3 (Thought \"invalid order\"). Similarly b-002 same.\n\nSo the pattern: \n- Each cycle: Thought, Action, Observation. Then next cycle starts with Thought.\n- Final can appear only at end, after an Observation? Actually after Observation is a reasoning boundary. But could Final appear after Thought? Probably not, because Final should be a conclusion, not part of cycle. The description: \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" A reasoning boundary is after an Observation? Or after a Thought? Typically ReAct: Thought, Action, Observation, then optionally Final. So Final after Observation is allowed. But after a Thought? Not a boundary because thought is internal. So Final must follow an Observation. In the examples, \"Final: done\" is last line, after a Thought? Actually in valid case b-000: lines: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Final. So after Observation, we have Thought, then Final. That violates? Let's examine: lines: \n1 Thought: inspect\n2 Action: lookup\n3 Observation: found\n4 Thought: answer\n5 Final: done\nThat sequence: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Final. According to rules, after Observation we should start a new cycle? Actually after Observation, the next can be Thought (starting next cycle) or Final? The description says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" A reasoning boundary could be after an Observation, because that's when you have new information. In the valid example, after Observation, we have a Thought (reasoning), then Final. But Final is not right after Observation, it's after Thought. That means the boundary is not strictly after Observation but after any reasoning? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the rules are:\n- Nonterminal cycle: Thought -> Action -> Observation (in order, no skips).\n- After Observation, you can either start a new cycle with Thought or end with Final.\n- Final must be the last line.\n- You cannot have multiple Thoughts consecutively (except maybe if it's part of cycle? No, only one Thought per cycle).\n- You cannot have Action after Action without Observation.\n- You cannot have Observation after Observation without Action.\n- You cannot have Final before the end.\n\nSo in valid case: \n1 T, 2 A, 3 O, 4 T, 5 F -> that's valid because after O, we have T (new cycle), then F. But F is not after an Observation directly. However, it's after a Thought. But the rule says \"only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" A reasoning boundary might be after a Thought as well? I'm not sure.\n\nLooking at invalid cases: \nb-001: T, A, T, T, F. The violation is at line 3 where after Action we have Thought instead of Observation. So the first violation is line 3. The valid prefix is lines 1-2.\n\nSo our algorithm: \n- Parse each line into type and text.\n- Keep state machine: expecting what next type.\n- Initial state: expecting Thought (or Final? But probably first must be Thought. If first is Final, that's invalid? The problem says \"Permit Final only at the end\". If first line is Final, that's not at end, so invalid. So initial expected: Thought.)\n- After Thought: expect Action (or maybe Action Input? But not present, so Action).\n- After Action: expect Observation (or maybe Action Input? But we don't have Action Input lines, so skip).\n- After Observation: expect either Thought (start new cycle) or Final (if it's the last line). Actually after Observation, you can have Thought or Final. But if you have Final, it must be last line. So we need to check that Final is last later.\n- After Final: nothing allowed, so if more lines after Final, invalid.\n- Also, if after Observation we get another Action, that's invalid.\n- After Action, we can't get Thought.\n- After Thought, we can't get Observation directly.\n\nAlso, what about multiple Thoughts in a row? That would be after a Thought, we expect Action, so second Thought would be invalid. In invalid b-001, after Action (line2), we have Thought (line3) - that's invalid because after Action we need Observation. Then line4 is Thought again. But the violation is at line3, so we stop.\n\nSo we iterate lines, maintain state, and check. Also need to check that Final appears only at the end and only after an Observation? Actually in valid example, Final after a Thought. But maybe it's allowed after a Thought if the previous was Observation? The sequence was T,A,O,T,F. So Final after Thought, but the thought is part of a new cycle that never completes? That might be acceptable if Final is the terminal. The rule says \"only at a reasoning boundary.\" Reasoning boundary could be after a Thought (when you decide to conclude). More likely they allow Final after a Thought as well, as long as it's the last line. But to be safe, we need to derive from description: \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" The reasoning boundary is usually after an Observation (when new information is incorporated). But in the valid example, Final is after a Thought. That suggests that a Thought can also be a reasoning boundary? Actually the thought \"answer\" is concluding. So maybe after a Thought, you can go directly to Final.\n\nLet's re-read: \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" In the valid case, the reasoning boundary is the last Thought? But the boundary might be at the start of a new cycle. Hard to tell.\n\nGiven the task is to implement based on given examples, we can infer that the valid case b-000 is valid, so our state machine should accept it. In b-000, after Observation (line3), we have Thought (line4) - that's allowed as start of new cycle. Then after Thought, we have Final (line5). So our state machine must allow Final after Thought. So state after Thought: either Action (continue cycle) or Final (if end). Similarly, after Observation: either Thought (next cycle) or Final (end). After Action: only Observation.\n\nAlso, what about after Final? No more lines.\n\nAlso, can we have Final after Action? No, because after Action you must have Observation.\n\nSo state machine:\n- states: START, THOUGHT, ACTION, OBSERVATION, FINAL\n- transitions:\n  - START -> THOUGHT (first line must be Thought) or maybe FINAL? But if first line is Final, that would be invalid because not at end? Actually if the only line is Final, it could be valid? But the description says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" If the whole trajectory is just Final, is that a reasoning boundary? Likely no. So we require at least one Thought? Better to follow the valid example: always start with Thought. So we'll enforce first line is Thought.\n  - THOUGHT -> ACTION (to continue cycle) or FINAL (to end, but must be last line)\n  - ACTION -> OBSERVATION\n  - OBSERVATION -> THOUGHT (next cycle) or FINAL (to end)\n  - FINAL -> (no transition, any further lines invalid)\n\nAlso, we need to track line numbers and collect records.\n\nWhen we detect a transition that is not allowed, we report violation with line number (1-based) and the valid prefix records up to the previous line.\n\nEdge cases: What if first line is not Thought? Then violation at line 1, valid prefix empty.\n\nWhat if final appears not at end? Violation at the line after final.\n\nAlso, need to parse lines: each line must match pattern: \"Thought:\" or \"Action:\" or \"Observation:\" or \"Final:\" followed by anything (including empty). Use regex or split at \": \"? The examples have \"Thought: inspect\". So split on first colon and space? But be careful: the text may contain colons? Probably not. We'll use `line.split(\": \", 1)` and check type is one of the four. If not, that's an invalid line? The problem says \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" So if a line doesn't match any, it might be a violation. But the fixture only has these. We'll treat any non-matching line as invalid? Possibly ignore? Better to treat as violation at that line because it's not a valid record. But the description says \"Parse only complete ... records\", meaning we should ignore incomplete ones? Actually it says \"Parse only complete ... records.\" That might mean we only consider lines that are complete records, but if a line is not a valid record, it's a violation. We'll treat as invalid line - violation.\n\nBut let's just implement as per examples.\n\nReturn object: We'll return a dict with keys:\n- \"valid\": bool\n- \"records\": list of dict with \"type\" and \"text\" (original text after colon? or the full line? Probably just the content)\n- \"violation_line\": int (only if invalid)\n- \"valid_prefix\": list of records up to the line before violation (or up to the violation line? \"valid prefix\" meaning the subsequence that is valid until violation. So it includes records before the violating line.)\n- Possibly \"line\" and \"valid_prefix\" as a separate dict? The description says \"return its one-based line and the valid prefix\". So we might have a \"violation\" object like {\"line\": , \"valid_prefix\": }. We'll do that.\n\nAlso need to return lowercase typed trajectory records. So type: \"thought\", \"action\", \"observation\", \"final\".\n\nNow, we also need to consider \"tool_schema\" and \"observation_binding\". The description says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: valid_turn_order, tool_schema, observation_binding, stop_semantics.\" But we have no additional data. So we only validate turn order and stop semantics (Final at end). Tool schema and observation binding might require that the action text matches some known tools? Not given. So we ignore them or assume any text is valid. Possibly the test cases will include invalid action names? But we have no schema, so we cannot validate. The problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" The required fields: type, text. We have that.\n\nGiven the fixture only provides lines, we should just parse and validate order.\n\nThus implementation:\n\n- Input case is a dict with key \"lines\" (list of strings).\n- We'll create empty records list, state = \"START\", valid = True, violation = None.\n- Iterate over lines with enumerate starting at 1.\n- For each line, parse type and text using split.\n- If split doesn't yield two parts or type not in set, set violation at current line, valid=False, break.\n- Else, check transition:\n   - if state == \"START\" and type != \"thought\": violation.\n   - elif state == \"THOUGHT\" and type not in {\"action\", \"final\"}: violation.\n   - elif state == \"ACTION\" and type != \"observation\": violation.\n   - elif state == \"OBSERVATION\" and type not in {\"thought\", \"final\"}: violation.\n   - elif state == \"FINAL\": violation (since final should be last).\n   - else: allowed transition. Update state to type (except start->thought becomes thought). Append record.\n- After loop, if no violation and state != \"FINAL\" and state != \"OBSERVATION\"? Actually must end with Final? The valid example ends with Final. The problem says \"Permit Final only at the end\". So if the last line is not Final, is that a violation? The trajectory should end with Final to be valid? Not necessarily? The description says \"Permit Final only at the end\" meaning it's allowed, but does it require it? It says \"Parse and validate interleaved ... under stop grammar\". The stop grammar likely requires a Final as terminal. In the invalid examples, they have final but earlier violations. In valid example, it ends with Final. So we should require that the last line is Final. If not, it's a violation. Also, after Observation, if the trajectory ends, that might be incomplete. So we'll require Final at end.\n- If no violation and last record type != \"final\": violation at line after last? Actually we can set violation at the end? Perhaps we should treat incomplete trajectory as invalid. So we'll add a check: if valid and state != \"FINAL\", set violation at a virtual line? But the problem says \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" If the trajectory is incomplete, the last line is actually valid, but the sequence is incomplete. We could say the violation occurs at the hypothetical next line? Or consider that the trajectory must end with Final. So if it doesn't, the last line (which is not Final) is the violation? In valid case, last is Final. So we'll check if the last record is Final; if not, set violation at the last line number (the line that should have been Final but wasn't). But the valid prefix would then be all records except the last? That seems off. Alternatively, we can treat it as invalid at the end, but we need to return a line number. Perhaps the valid prefix includes all records, and the violation line is beyond the last line? Not clear. Since the fixture only has valid case ending with Final, and invalid ones also have Final but earlier violation, we can assume that the trajectory must end with Final. If it doesn't, we return violation at the last line (the line that is not Final) with valid prefix up to the previous line. That seems consistent: last line is the violation because it should be Final. We'll implement that.\n\nAlso, we need to handle \"Action Input\" lines? Not present.\n\nFinally, if case is structurally invalid (e.g., lines is not a list, or missing key), raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get(\"lines\")\n    if not isinstance(lines, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing or invalid 'lines'\")\n    valid = True\n    records = []\n    state = \"START\"\n    violation_line = None\n    valid_prefix = []  # will be records up to violation\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\n        if \":\" not in line:\n            # invalid line\n            violation_line = idx\n            valid = False\n            break\n        prefix, sep, text = line.partition(\":\")\n        # allow colon after prefix? e.g., \"Thought: inspect\"\n        # but prefix might have spaces? e.g., \"Thought\" exactly.\n        prefix = prefix.strip().lower()\n        if prefix not in {\"thought\", \"action\", \"observation\", \"final\"}:\n            violation_line = idx\n            valid = False\n            break\n        # text is after colon; if there's a space after colon, text might start with space.\n        text = text.lstrip()  # remove leading space\n        type = prefix\n        # check transition\n        if state == \"START\":\n            if type != \"thought\":\n                violation_line = idx\n                break\n            state = \"thought\"\n        elif state == \"thought\":\n            if type not in {\"action\", \"final\"}:\n                violation_line = idx\n                break\n            if type == \"final\":\n                # Final must be last line. We'll check later.\n                pass\n            state = type\n        elif state == \"action\":\n            if type != \"observation\":\n                violation_line = idx\n                break\n            state = \"observation\"\n        elif state == \"observation\":\n            if type not in {\"thought\", \"final\"}:\n                violation_line = idx\n                break\n            if type == \"final\":\n                # check if this is last line? We'll check later.\n                pass\n            state = type\n        elif state == \"final\":\n            # Already final, any more lines is violation\n            violation_line = idx\n            break\n        records.append({\"type\": type, \"text\": text})\n        if not valid:\n            break\n    if not valid:\n        # violation_line set\n        valid_prefix = records  # records before the violation line\n        # Actually records already include lines up to previous line? Need to exclude violating line.\n        # We broke before appending the violating line, so records contains previous lines.\n        # Set valid_prefix to records.\n    else:\n        # Check that last record is final\n        if not records or records[-1][\"type\"] != \"final\":\n            # violation at last line (the line that caused the mismatch)\n            # If records empty, violation at line 1? But first line must be thought.\n            if records:\n                violation_line = len(lines)  # should be last line number\n                # But we need to indicate that the last line is invalid because it's not final.\n                # We can treat the last line as the offending line. However, we already processed it and it passed transition? Actually if it was not final, it passed state transition but then we later check. We'll set violation_line to len(lines) and set valid_prefix to records[:-1] (since last record is the violation). But we must ensure we didn't break early.\n            else:\n                violation_line = 1  # no records, first line missing?\n                valid_prefix = []\n            valid = False\n        # else valid\n\n    if valid:\n        return {\"valid\": True, \"records\": records}\n    else:\n        return {\"valid\": False, \"records\": records, \"violation\": {\"line\": violation_line, \"valid_prefix\": valid_prefix}}\n\nBut need to be careful: In case of invalid line, we break before appending. So records are the valid prefix. That's fine.\n\nEdge: In the case where records is empty and first line is invalid, violation_line=1, valid_prefix=[].\n\nNow, we need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The Python source must be a string. We'll provide the function code.\n\nAlso note: \"Do not mutate the input.\" We read case but don't modify.\n\nNow, write the implementation as a Python function. Ensure it's self-contained with no imports.\n\nWe'll include docstring? Not required. Just function.\n\nWe'll escape newlines and quotes for JSON string.\n\nLet's writ